Policy: Data Classification
   1. Purpose
   The purpose of the Destination Vancouver Data Classification Policy (the “Policy”) is to establish a consistent framework for identifying,
   classifying, and protecting the organization's data assets. This Policy provides a clear and consistent structure for categorizing data based on its
   level of sensitivity, value, and risk to the organization.
   This classification system enables informed decisions about how data can be stored, shared, and used — including what data may be safely
   uploaded to or extracted by artificial intelligence (“AI”) tools.
   By implementing this Policy, we aim to:
           ▪   Safeguard information from unauthorized access, use, or disclosure.
           ▪   Ensure compliance with legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including British Columbia's Personal Information Protection
               Act (PIPA).
           ▪   Empower Destination Vancouver employees, contractors and volunteers (collectively, “Team Members”) to handle data responsibly
               and make informed decisions about its use with new technologies.
           ▪   Support and supplement the expectations established in Destination Vancouver’s Artificial Intelligence Driven Tools Policy Data
At Destination Vancouver, data refers to any piece of information that is collected, created, received, stored, processed, or shared in the course of
our work — regardless of format or system.
Data can exist as:
   ▪   A record (e.g., an entry in a CRM or database);
   ▪   A document’s content (e.g., a report, strategic plan, or contract); or
   ▪   A file or object (e.g., image, audio clip, email, chat message).

Data may be ‘structured’ (e.g., spreadsheets, forms, tables) or unstructured (e.g., PDFs, Word documents, presentation slides, notes, emails,
recorded calls, or freeform text in meeting tools).




