Examples of data include, but are not limited to:
   ▪   Meeting minutes in Word
   ▪   Email correspondence with partners
   ▪   A PDF report from a vendor
   ▪   A photo uploaded to Box or official social media channels
   ▪   Budget numbers in an Excel file
   ▪   Client records in SimpleView CRM
   ▪   Internal chat conversations in 8x8 or MS Teams
   ▪   Research data stored by third-party providers

Each of these may contain content that is subject to classification under this Policy depending on its purpose, audience, and risk.

   2. Scope
   This Policy applies to all data created, received, stored, processed, or shared by Team Members and authorized partners in the course of
   business.
   The scope encompasses all organizational data, regardless of its format (digital or physical), across both structured systems (e.g., CRM
   platforms like SimpleView) and unstructured sources (e.g., emails, chat messages, meeting notes, documents, audio, and video files).
   The Policy covers data stored in organization-managed systems such as Microsoft Outlook, Teams, Box, and 8x8, as well as any data shared
   with third-party service providers or accessed via AI tools. This classification framework also informs what data may be extracted or inputted
   using integrated AI tools in the future. This classification framework is intended to support day-to-day operational use and to guide decision-
   making related to AI tool experimentation, enterprise tool adoption, and responsible data sharing.

   3. Roles and responsibilities
           a. Data Owners
               The Director Leadership Team (DLT) or designated Team Members are Data Owners for the data assets created and managed by
               their teams. Their responsibilities include:
                   •   Classifying all data assets within their purview according to this Policy in the Data Inventory & Classification Report.
                   •   Reviewing data classifications periodically to ensure they remain accurate.
                   •   Communicating any changes in data sensitivity to Destination Vancouver’s Privacy Officer.
Destination Vancouver Data Classification Policy – January 9, 2026
                                                                                                                                                     2
           b. Policy oversight
               Destination Vancouver’s Privacy Officer has oversight for this document, with overall responsibility for its implementation and
               maintenance. Their responsibilities include:
                  •   Administering this Policy and developing related procedures.
                  •   Coordinating training for all Team Members on data handling best practices.
                  •   Supporting Data Owners in applying the classification framework.
                  •   Ensuring organizational compliance with relevant privacy regulations like PIPA.
           c. AI Council (AI Strategic Oversight)
               The AI Council provides strategic oversight to ensure this Policy aligns with the organization's AI governance objectives. Their
               responsibilities include:
                  •   Reviewing this Policy annually to ensure it effectively addresses AI-related risks and opportunities.
                  •   Advising on how data classification impacts the approval and use of AI tools.
                  •   Recommending updates to the classification framework as AI technologies evolve.
           d. All Team Members
               Every Team Member is responsible for protecting the data they handle in their daily work. Responsibilities include:
                  •   Understanding and applying the correct data classifications.
                  •   Following all handling, storage, and sharing rules for each data tier.
                  •   Reporting any potential data mishandling or Policy violations immediately.




